    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado's 2026 legislative session produced 32 bills, and the ones that touch AI directly are mostly about how it is used on people, not how it is built.\n\nThe AI-specific bills cluster around health care, psychotherapy, conversational services, and automated decision-making. SB189 on automated decision-making technology and HB1263 on conversational AI operator requirements are the broadest of these. HB1195 restricts AI in psychotherapy and HB1139 governs AI in health care. The pattern is sector-by-sector rules for AI that makes decisions about people, with no single comprehensive statute covering the technology as a whole.\n\nThe compute and power side appears in the same session but as a separate track. SB102 on large-load data centers and HB1030 on data center and utility modernization are the two bills that address the physical layer directly. HB1326 sunsets the Public Utilities Commission and HB1246 concerns consumer-regulated electric utilities, both of which sit adjacent to the question of who pays for the grid capacity data centers draw. A local news item from September 2026 reports a move to prohibit AI data centers throughout the city of Linden, which shows the siting fight reaching the municipal level.\n\nWhat this data does not show is outcomes. It lists bill numbers, titles, and dates, not votes, amendments, or whether anything was signed, vetoed, or died in committee. It also does not say how many of the 32 bills are actually about AI, since several concern data privacy, social media, or law enforcement surveillance without naming AI. The three news items are too few to establish a trend on their own. Read the bill list as a map of where Colorado legislators chose to act, not as a record of what became law.",
